WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 262: Ralph Waldo Emerson: Nature, Self-Reliance, and the American Scholar

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.840
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the luminous fountainhead of American thought.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:22.480
[Rebecca] The conquered essayist, poet, and lecturer who declared our intellectual independence and urged every soul to trust its own divine spark.

5
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.480
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Ralph Waldo Emerson.

6
00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:40.160
[George] Born in Boston in May 1803, Ralph Waldo Emerson was born into a prestigious dynasty of Massachusetts Puritan and Unitarian clergymen stretching back seven generations.

7
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:55.440
[George] When his minister father died when Ralph was only eight years old, his brilliant, eccentric Calvinist Aunt Mary Moody Emerson stepped in to nurture his intellectual ambition, famously challenging the impoverished boy to do what you are afraid to do.

8
00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:04.640
[George] Entering Harvard at 14, Emerson worked as a waiter in the College Commons to pay his tuition, beginning his lifelong wide world journal.

9
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:19.360
[George] In 1829, 26-year-old Emerson was ordained junior pastor of the Second Church of Boston, the historic pulpit once occupied by Cotton Mather, and married his radiant, 19-year-old bride Ellen Lewis A Tucker.

10
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:27.600
[George] But their blissful domestic paradise was shattered just 18 months later when Ellen died of tuberculosis in February 1831.

11
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:35.600
[George] Emerson was utterly devastated by grief, walking every morning from Boston to her tomb in Roxbury, crushed by loss.

12
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.200
[George] Emerson experienced a profound spiritual crisis.

13
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:52.720
[George] Finding the formal rituals of the church hollow, he refused to administer the sacrament of the Lord's Supper, resigning his prestigious pulpit in 1832 and boarding a sailing ship for Europe to find meaning in the wider world.

14
00:01:52.720 --> 00:02:06.920
[George] Raised in poverty by a heroic widowed mother and his eccentric, brilliant Aunt Mary Moody Emerson, young Ralph learned early to cultivate an independent, disciplined inner life grounded in moral duty and intellectual curiosity.

15
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:22.120
[George] The tragic death of his 19-year-old wife Ellen in 1831 shattered his traditional theological assumptions, compelling him to abandon the formal rituals of the Unitarian pulpit to seek direct spiritual revelation in the wider universe.

16
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.920
[George] Emerson's European voyage was a transformative intellectual resurrection.

17
00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:46.360
[George] In England and Scotland, he sought out the literary titans of the age, climbing the rugged hills of Crajan Puttock to visit the brooding Scottish historian Thomas Carlyle, forming a lifelong, affectionate friendship that spanned four decades, and dining with William Wordsworth and Samuel Taylor Coleridge in the Lake District.

18
00:02:46.360 --> 00:03:04.120
[George] In July 1833, while touring the Jardin des Plants in Paris, Emerson walked through the cabinet of natural history, gazing with breathless awe at thousands of preserved botanical specimens, fossil shells, and minerals arranged in an evolutionary continuum by George's Covier.

19
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:13.880
[George] Struck by an electrifying epiphany, Emerson realized that an unbroken, living harmony connected the physical laws of nature to the moral laws of the human spirit.

20
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.280
[George] I am moved by strange sympathies.

21
00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:19.400
[George] I say continually, I will be a naturalist.

22
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:30.920
[George] Returning to Massachusetts in 1834, Emerson settled in the historic village of Concord, purchasing a stately white clapboard house known as Bush along the Cambridge Turnpike.

23
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:40.120
[George] Marrying Lydia Jackson, whom he affectionately renamed Lydian, and resolving to reinvent himself as an independent philosopher and public lecturer.

24
00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:54.800
[George] Emerson's transformative journey through Europe and his epiphany at the Jardin des Plants in Paris revealed to him the sublime correspondence between the natural order and the human soul, convincing him that nature was a living language of divine spirit.

25
00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:04.800
[George] Settling in Concord at his beloved estate Bush, Emerson created an intellectual sanctuary that became the philosophical capital of 19th century America.

26
00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:13.760
[George] In September 1836, 33-year-old Emerson published anonymously a slim 95-page book titled Simply Nature.

27
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.760
[George] It was the founding manifesto of American transcendentalism.

28
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:29.840
[George] Emerson challenged Americans to stop living on the dry dogmas and faded memories of dead European ancestors, asking boldly, why should not we also enjoy an original relation to the universe?

29
00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.560
[George] Why should not we have a poetry and philosophy of insight and not of tradition?

30
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.800
[George] And a religion by revelation to us, and not the history of theirs.

31
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:49.680
[George] In a famous ecstatic passage describing a solitary walk across a muddy conquered common at twilight, Emerson articulated the mystical union between the observer and the cosmos.

32
00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:58.240
[George] Standing on the bare ground, my head bathed by the blithe air and uplifted into infinite space, all mean egotism vanishes.

33
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:05.760
[George] I become a transparent eyeball, I am nothing, I see all, the currents of the universal being circulate through me.

34
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.240
[George] I am part or parcel of God.

35
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:23.360
[George] Nature ignited a cultural revolution, gathering a brilliant circle of conquered intellectuals, including Margaret Fuller, Henry David Thoreau, and Bronson Alcott, to found the Transcendental Club and publish their revolutionary journal, The Dial.

36
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:37.160
[George] The publication of Nature in 1836 struck like a thunderbolt in American culture, inviting a young nation to cast off European scholastic dogmas and forge an original, direct relation to the divine cosmos.

37
00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:48.680
[George] His vision of the transparent eyeball captured the ecstatic experience of mystical union with the oversoul, dissolving the boundaries between the solitary human soul and the infinite universe.

38
00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:59.400
[George] On August 31, 1837, Emerson delivered the annual Phi Beta Kappa oration before the faculty and students of Harvard College, the American scholar.

39
00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:05.480
[George] Oliver Wendell Holmes later famously hailed the speech as our intellectual declaration of independence.

40
00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:21.240
[George] Emerson declared that the American scholar must not be a timid, bookworm parrot of foreign traditions, but man-thinking, an independent spirit who draws wisdom directly from the living book of nature, from active physical labor in the world, and from self-trust.

41
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:33.080
[George] Eleven months later, on a sweltering July evening in 1838, Emerson addressed the graduating class of the Harvard Divinity School, delivering his explosive divinity school address.

42
00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:45.880
[George] Emerson sharply attacked traditional Orthodox Christianity as a frozen corpse, condemning the church for worshiping historical miracles rather than experiencing the living presence of the divine in the human heart today.

43
00:06:45.880 --> 00:07:01.960
[George] Conservative Boston clergymen were outraged, the dean of the school denounced Emerson as an atheist infidel, and Emerson was effectively banned from the Harvard campus for thirty years, yet the younger generation of students cheered him as their liberating spiritual hero.

44
00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:17.520
[George] The American scholar and the controversial Divinity School Address challenged the intellectual establishment of Harvard College, urging American thinkers to stop imitating foreign models and instead draw inspiration from the living wellsprings of nature and self-trust.

45
00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:28.720
[George] Despite being ostracized by conservative Boston clergymen, Emerson's message electrified a generation of young idealists, including Thoreau, Fuller, and Hawthorne.

46
00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:37.840
[George] In 1841, Emerson published essays, first series, containing his most famous and influential essay, Self-Reliance.

47
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:46.560
[George] It was a thundering, revolutionary trumpet call to individual conscience against the suffocating pressures of social conformity and herd mentality.

48
00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.360
[George] Emerson delivered immortal maxims that became etched into the American character.

49
00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.440
[George] Trust thyself, every heart vibrates to that iron string.

50
00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:58.560
[George] Whoso would be a man must be a nonconformist.

51
00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.160
[George] A foolish consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds.

52
00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.000
[George] Adored by little statesmen and philosophers and divines.

53
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:22.560
[George] Emerson argued that every human being possesses a unique, divine spark implanted by the oversoul, the universal spiritual consciousness flowing through all life, and that copying another person or conforming to social fashion is a form of spiritual suicide.

54
00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:38.800
[George] He urged his listeners to stand tall, speak their truth boldly even if the whole world misunderstands them, reminding them that to be great is to be misunderstood, just as Socrates, Jesus, Copernicus, Galileo, and Newton had been misunderstood before them.

55
00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:52.800
[George] In self-reliance, Emerson delivered an immortal manifesto of personal nonconformity, commanding every individual to trust their own divine intuition and resist the deadening pressures of social conformity and herd opinion.

56
00:08:52.800 --> 00:09:03.720
[George] His piercing maxims, warning that imitation is suicide, and that every heart vibrates to the iron string of self-trust, became foundational principles of the American character.

57
00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:16.040
[George] For three decades, Emerson was the preeminent star of the American Lyceum Lecture Circuit, traveling tens of thousands of miles across the country by rail, stagecoach, and frozen sleigh.

58
00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:29.640
[George] In drafty Midwestern town halls, Chicago churches and frontier theaters, farmers, merchants and factory workers paid 25 cents to hear the sage of Concord lecture on character, poetry and power.

59
00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:38.280
[George] Possessing a warm, resonant baritone voice and an aura of radiant serenity, Emerson uplifted millions of ordinary Americans.

60
00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:49.480
[George] When Congress passed the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, requiring Northern citizens to capture escaping enslaved people, Emerson's serene detachment vanished in outrage.

61
00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:51.320
[George] He wrote in his journal.

62
00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:56.760
[George] This filthy enactment was made in the 19th century by people who could read and write.

63
00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:59.160
[George] I will not obey it by God.

64
00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:12.600
[George] Emerson threw his massive prestige into the abolitionist crusade, delivering impassioned anti-slavery speeches, raising funds for John Brown, and welcoming Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman into his conquered parlor.

65
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:28.360
[George] As a tireless lecturer on the National Lyceum Circuit, Emerson carried his gospel of optimism, character, and intellectual freedom into frontier schoolhouses, city auditoriums and Midwest town halls, uplifting millions of ordinary citizens.

66
00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:41.240
[George] In the struggle against human slavery, Emerson abandoned his philosophical detachment, fiercely denouncing the fugitive slave act and standing beside Frederick Douglass and John Brown in the fight for human liberty.

67
00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:48.160
[George] In his final years, Emerson's magnificent memory gradually faded into a peaceful, gentle twilight.

68
00:10:48.160 --> 00:11:04.240
[George] In 1872, when his beloved Concord House caught fire, hundreds of neighbors and admirers from across the nation secretly raised $12,000 to rebuild it and sent Emerson on an all-expenses paid grand tour of Egypt, Europe, and England.

69
00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:13.440
[George] Returning home, he was greeted at the Concord train station by church bells, school bands, and cheering crowds who escorted him to his restored study.

70
00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:20.400
[George] When Henry Wadsworth Longfellow died in 1882, a frail Emerson stood beside his coffin and whispered.

71
00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:34.160
[George] One month later, on April 27, 1882, 78-year-old Ralph Waldo Emerson died peacefully of pneumonia.

72
00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:45.440
[George] He was laid to rest on Authors Ridge in Sleepy Hollow Cemetery in Concord, surrounded by his friends Thoreau, Hawthorne, and the Alcott's, beneath an uncarved pink quartz boulder.

73
00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.560
[George] Emerson did not leave behind a rigid philosophical system or dogmatic creed.

74
00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:57.600
[George] He left behind a radiant spirit of intellectual courage, optimism, and boundless faith in human potential.

75
00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:04.320
[George] The philosopher who taught America to stand upon its own feet, think its own thoughts, and walk toward the light.

76
00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:17.040
[George] Emerson's final years in Concord were illuminated by the loving devotion of his family, neighbors, and an admiring nation, who recognized in the gentle sage the moral architect of American intellectual freedom.

77
00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:27.920
[George] His uncarved pink quartz monument in Sleepy Hollow Cemetery remains a sacred pilgrimage site, honoring the thinker who taught humanity to trust the divine light within.

78
00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.640
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

